-- Named rejection: recursion that is not SELF-reference.
--
-- Both self-reference checks are local — the structural recogniser certifies a
-- decreasing list argument within one definition, and the generic path rejects
-- any other call to the definition's own name — so neither can see a cycle that
-- runs through a second definition. Two decisions that call each other lowered
-- cleanly, reported `stratified`, and looped forever in every Prolog-family
-- target: an out-of-fragment construct that lowers, which the M1 brief calls a
-- bug in the same breath as an in-fragment one that does not.
--
-- Read this beside ../unstratified.l4, which is the same SHAPE and lowers
-- successfully on purpose. The difference is the sign: that cycle runs through
-- a negation, so the Apt-Blair-Walker result already records it and each leg
-- decides for itself (ASP wants it, swipl and Blawx reject it). This one is
-- purely positive, so stratification says nothing about it and there would be
-- no other warning anywhere. The middle-end reports exactly the cycles nothing
-- else reports, and no others.

@export
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `p holds` IF `q holds` n

@export
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `q holds` IF `p holds` n
